# /etc/fineco/alert.env  — live-refresh alerting config (deploy/alerting/fineco-alert.sh).
#
# Read by the fineco-alert unit/timer, which runs as ROOT. Install 0640 root:root.
# Optional: the timer runs with the defaults below even if this file is absent.
# NEVER commit a filled-in copy.
#
# The alerts are payload-free (alert TYPE + a COUNT + a timestamp) — they never
# carry a value — so the channel you pick here only ever sees operational signals.

# How to deliver a fired alert. The script pipes ONE line to `sh -c "$FINECO_ALERT_COMMAND"`
# on stdin. Default (if unset): journald via `logger -t fineco-alert`.
#
# SECURITY — DO NOT put a secret (webhook token, API key, password) DIRECTLY in
# FINECO_ALERT_COMMAND. The command runs as a normal process, so its arguments are
# visible in process listings (`ps`, /proc/<pid>/cmdline) to local users while it
# runs — which would defeat this file's 0640 mode. Have your notifier read its
# secret from a FILE instead (these keep the token out of argv):
#   - curl:   --config <file>   (put `header = "Authorization: Bearer …"` there),
#             or  -H @<file>,  or  --netrc-file <file>   (file 0600 root:root)
#   - email:  `mail`/msmtp read their relay credentials from their OWN config
#
# Examples (pick ONE). Ready-to-adapt notifier configs + the full walkthrough are in
# deploy/alerting/examples/ and docs/SELF-HOSTING.md ("Alerting notifications"):
#   FINECO_ALERT_COMMAND='logger -t fineco-alert'                        # journald (default, no secret)
#   FINECO_ALERT_COMMAND='curl -fsS -o /dev/null --config /etc/fineco/telegram.curl --data-urlencode text@-'  # Telegram
#   FINECO_ALERT_COMMAND='curl -fsS -o /dev/null --config /etc/fineco/ntfy.curl -d @-'                          # ntfy
#   FINECO_ALERT_COMMAND='mail -s "fineco-helper alert" you@example.com'                           # email (msmtp)
# For the curl/mail channels the SECRET (bot token / ntfy token / SMTP password)
# lives in the referenced 0600 root:root config file, NEVER here or in argv. Note
# `-f`: curl must EXIT NON-ZERO on an HTTP 4xx/5xx, else a failed send is counted as
# delivered and the alert would not re-fire.
FINECO_ALERT_COMMAND='logger -t fineco-alert'

# Thresholds — alert when the count of NEW matching events in one scan window
# (the timer interval, ~3 min) reaches N. Defaults shown; raise to reduce noise.
#   EGRESS: a single denied worker packet is a real signal, but a brief cold-start
#   window before the egress set first populates can produce benign denies — raise
#   if that is noisy on your host.
# FINECO_ALERT_EGRESS_MIN=1
# FINECO_ALERT_GATEWAY_EGRESS_MIN=1   # gateway exfil-attempt egress denies
# FINECO_ALERT_AUTHFAIL_MIN=3
# FINECO_ALERT_SPIKE_MIN=6
# FINECO_ALERT_RESTART_MIN=3
